into the words as an adaptation of  Stephen Sondheim and James Lapine's Tony  award-winning stage musical which blends  a bunch of Brothers Grimm fairy tales  and throws an original story into the  mix for good measure the classic fairy  tales they get a modern twist of  Cinderella Jack in the Beanstalk Little  Red Riding Hood and Rapunzel while the  new story that links them all together  follows a childless Baker in his wife as  they tried to reverse a curse placed on  them by Wicked Witch now I love a good  musical whether that's classic like  singing in the rain Seven Brides for  Seven Brothers Calamity Jane meet me in  st. Louis and many more or modern movies  like Chicago lame is enchanted once all  pitch perfect  now this isn't director Bob Marshalls  first all-star musical though his  success rates been somewhat hit and miss  there was the brilliant oscar-winning  movie adaptation of candor and ebbs  Chicago with Catherine zeta-jones Renee  Zellweger and Richard Gere then there  was also the not quite so brilliant 9  which was even more star-studded with  Daniel day-lewis Kate Hudson Judi Dench  Mary Ann co-ceo Sophia Loren Nicole  Kidman Penelope Cruz and Fergie from The  Black Eyed Peas few but Marshalls  musical experience doesn't end there  he won a Primetime Emmy for his  choreography on the TV movie of Annie  which he also directed and he's  choreographed movie musicals including  victor/victoria with Julie Andrews and  stage musicals such as cabaret so  basically he's got movie musical form  I've not seen the original stage version  of into the woods but it's been rather  controversial that Disney was adapting  it especially in terms of what they'd  cut and wasn't a musicals tone would  suffer this movie musical is already  getting awards nominations though  including Golden Globes  so does into the woods work as a movie  well it certainly gets you right into  the fairytale mood from the start as it  kicks off with the line once upon a time  in a far-off Kingdom the lines spoken by  an unseen James Corden who plays the  Baker we hear his narration in fact here  and there throughout the film though the  voiceovers never overbearing as it's  used sparingly and fits neatly with the  fairytale setting the Brothers Grimm  fairy tales and Sondheim's musical on  which this film are based are both  pretty dark and although this is a PG  movie from Disney it still manages to  stay on the darkish side okay so it's  not Tim Burton's Sweeney Todd which had  an 18 certificate in the UK and an R  rating in the U  I kind of wonder if at what Tim Burton  would have done with this material  anyway in this into the woods you don't  actually see any violence or bloodshed  on screen with a camera cutting away  before anything nasty happens but while  things aren't necessarily shown on  screen they're certainly not left  totally to our imagination either as  it's pretty clear what's going to happen  so although the violence doesn't feature  explicitly on screen the movie still  feels suitably dark and remember the  movie screenplay was actually written by  james lepine who wrote the book for the  original musical - mixing together  characters and separate fairy tales  makes for some intriguing interactions  and some lovely comic moments - with  some particular standout parts and early  scenes between Little Red Riding Hood  and the Baker in the woods the only  characters I felt were a bit on the  bland side were Rapunzel and her prince  neither is given an awful lot of screen  time and neither has much to do when  they are on screen and there's a thread  in Rapunzel's story that's just kind of  left hanging  having said that Rapunzel's Prince does  have a very funny scene with his brother  Prince Charming and he gets to deliver a  nicely comic line to Rapunzel and  they're all over halfway through the  movie as inventive and witty and clever  as Stephen Sondheim's songs are and into  the woods and they very much are they  totally exist within the setting of the  musical I mean by that they're not  really the kind of tunes you'll find  yourself leaving the cinema singing  whistling or humming as you go as for  the look of the movie the sets the  costumes and most of the effects I  really cannot fault the behind the  scenes crew who do a great job acting  wise into the woods certainly both a  star-studded cast Meryl Streep is just  fabulous as the witch that Streep can  sing is no surprise now of course as  we've seen her sing on screen in the  full-on musical Mamma Mia postcards from  the edge and in Robert Altman's A  Prairie Home Companion as well and just  as you'd expect from an actress of  Streep's caliber she really makes you  believe her which he acts sounds and  looks the part her costume and makeup  look incredible too she's got this bluey  gray matted hair crooked blackened teeth  she's stooped over she's got these long  thick yellowed fingernails and this  ashen of face covered in wrinkles and  it's no wonder given her performance  that she's been nominated for Best  Supporting Actress  in a musical or comedy at the Golden  Globes Anna Kendrick's really got the  musical mania hasn't she what with movie  musicals pitch perfect 2 and the last  five years both out in 2015 that woman  just cannot stop singing and she really  hits the right note as Cinderella and  there's a lovely attention to detail too  in her costumes whether she's in her  scrubbing the floor outfit or kitted out  in a golden dress for the king's  festival Chris Pine gives a cracking  performance of Cinderella's Prince  really capturing his vanity and  arrogance in a lovely comic turn he's  already flexed his comedic muscles  recently in Horrible Bosses 2 we put on  a hilariously unhinged show and he gets  to develop that into the woods with some  lovely scenes with his brother who  simply called Rapunzel's Prince lookout  in particular for the song agony were  they desperately trying to outdo each  other Emily Blunt reunites with her  Devil Wears Prada co-star Meryl Streep  in a rather different role though she's  still treated pretty poorly by Luke's  character Blunt plays the Baker's wife  who's dying to have a baby but hasn't  been able to if you've seen her in  anything from looper to the five-year  engagement or the young victoria you'll  know Blunt is a fantastic actress but do  you also know she can sing well she can  and very well - thank you very much in  fact this isn't her first time belting  out a song on screen she actually won a  Golden Globe for her role in the BBC  drama called Gideon's daughter where she  also had to carry a tune  anyway I love her in into the woods for  the same reason I love her in just about  any role you always believe her and  whatever she's playing and she's been  nominated for Best Actress in a Musical  or Comedy at the Golden Globes - James  Corden is great as the Baker portraying  his character with ease you may know him  for writing and stirring and hip British  comedy Gavin and Stacey and of course  he's going to be replacing Craig  Ferguson as host of The Late Late Show  in 2015 again this isn't his first  musical venture he starred as amateur  opera singer and Britain's Got Talent  winner Paul Potts in the movie one  chance and going back to the late 90s he  played the lead singer in a boy band in  the TV comedy boys unlimited which was  actually co-written by David Walliams  and Matt Lucas Johnny Depp puts in a  brief appearance as the lascivious wolf  Depp's no stranger to Sondheim musicals  having starred in Tim Burton's movie  version of Sweeney Todd the Demon Barber  of Fleet Street and he's also worked  with into the woods director Rob  Marshall  for on the 4th Pirates of the Caribbean  movie on Stranger Tides but I'll try not  to hold that against him I love the  understated way they've styled his wolf  he's got a fedora hat with little ears  poking out a 1940's inspired gray blue  fur jacket over similarly colored shirt  and tie bushy eyebrows mustache and oh  yeah a tail and I love the  matter-of-fact interaction between his  wolf and Red Riding and it's also  perfectly natural when they meet good  day young ladies good day mr. wolf which  I think adds the Cundall kimura's  Christine Baranski play Cinderella's  stepmother again she's got musical form  she works alongside Streep in Mamma Mia  with into the woods director of martial  on the movie Chicago and she starred in  various productions on stage though she  doesn't have a lot to do in the movie  what she does do she does very well so  to sum up as far as Rob Marshall's  musicals go I still prefer Chicago but  into the woods is an enjoyably dark tale  with a sense of humor great performances  and gorgeous sets and costumes into the  woods gets 4 out of 5 flicks now I want  to know what did you think of into the  woods have you seen the stage musical  how do you think the movie compares and  watch your favorite movie musicals let  me know in the comments below and don't  forget to like this video and subscribe  for more of my weekly film reviews and  interviews thanks for watching and see  you next time  yippee-ki-yay movie lovers  you

